      "source_text": "Business wars 01 it's after 10 pm on december 14 2007 in a suburb outside of cleveland richard and thelma payne both in their 80s are getting ready for bed thelma's in the bathroom brushing her teeth their white split level house isn't fancy but they've lived here for more than 50 years and still love the place they raised six children here for thelma it's full of memories thelma spits out her toothpaste and turns on the water to rinse off her toothbrush not again richard richard shuffles in his pajama shirt half-buttoned what is it the water's being strange again richard turns the tap back on and watches the water sputter on and off air must have gotten into the pipes i'll call the plumber in the morning all right richard climbs into bed pulling back the covers for thelma she climbs in beside him and gives him a good night kiss good night dear good night sleep tight don't let the bed bugs bite and with that thelma drifts off to sleep sometime later in the middle of the night she's jolted awake what was that what's happening richard sits up listening carefully it sounds like all the drawers in the next room are sliding out and dropping onto the floor i think someone's ransacking the house before thelma can respond a large crack appears in the bedroom wall in seconds it spreads up into the ceiling richard this isn't a thief i know we've got to get out of here right now richard climbs out of bed and groans as he awkwardly twists his foot oh careful the floorboards are splitting the floor underneath them isn't just splitting it's caving in thelma looks down the hall and sees that their living room seems to have dropped several feet their belongings fly off the shelves as richard and thelma find a path along the ruins of their floor it smells like gas hurry thelma follows her husband out into the frigid night air and hurries with him across the street to safety they turn back to look at their house and gasp it's been knocked off its foundation and collapsed over to one side through the cracked foundation they can see wisps of blue flames dancing up from the ground below richard pulls them close as she starts to cry her house oh god what what happened i don't know honey something big thelma squeezes her husband tight wondering if she'll ever return to their home of half a century again the fire department determines that a natural gas leak is what caused the explosion that destroyed thelma and richard payne's home and it's not just the pains that are affected over the next 24 hours 19 homes along their suburban street have to be evacuated due to gas leaks nine months later the ohio department of natural resources identifies the source of those leaks a faulty natural gas well that had been drilled nearby using a technique most residents have never heard of that technique is called fracking from wondry i'm stephen johnson and this is american innovations when people talk about disrupters they generally mean the leaders from the world of high tech people like mark zuckerberg elon musk and steve jobs they aren't referring to moguls from the fossil fuel industry which is based on technologies that in some cases are nearly a century old but sometimes disruption comes not from new technology but from using old technology in new ways hydraulic fracturing or fracking is an extraction technique in which a mixture of water sand and chemicals is blasted at high power down a narrow well to break open the rock and free the oil or gas stored inside it was first invented in the 1940s but largely dismissed as too expensive and complicated to be of much use then in the 1970s american oil wells began drying up the big energy conglomerates shifted more of their production over seeds but the small independent energy companies didn't have that option if small companies wanted to survive the shift to foreign oil they would need to find more domestic sources of energy they'd find those sources in a deep layer of rock called shale which is rich in natural gas and they'd find the means of extracting that gas in an old technology fracking by the middle of the 1990s the fracking revolution was underway it would transform the american fossil fuel industry and make a lot of people very rich it was led not by the industry powerhouses like exxon and bp but by maverick independent companies that saw potential for natural gas where few others did but fracking would also become one of the most controversial innovations in american history proponents pitch fracking is good for the environment because it's most commonly used to extract natural gas which produces less carbon dioxide than coal and other fossil fuels when it's burned but environmental activists counter that fracking causes groundwater contamination makes people sick and contributes to global warming by releasing methane into the atmosphere they also argue that it delays the transition to renewable energy sources and extends our dependence on fossil fuels at a time when the burning of fossil fuels may be jeopardizing the future of our planet but before these big picture debates got underway fracking shale was a small enterprise spearheaded by one man george p mitchell he gave little thought to fracking's broader ramifications he was just trying to save his company this is the first episode in our three-part series on fracking the source rock we talk a lot on our show about how technology has evolved over time but for work software it seems like from the beginning they were all rigidly built for specific teams and functionality think about it other arenas of tech have become more innovative but works software's fundamentals have remained mostly unchanged in the last few decades that's why i was thrilled to find monday.com work os we use it to manage everything we're working on because it's an all-in-one platform that adapts to how we want to work not the other way around for managing internal processes to planning and tracking long-term goals and collaborating across teams monday.com work os feels like a futuristic way we should all work no matter the size of your team you can create workflow from scratch or pick a template get started right away and adjust it however you want also managers have full visibility into what their team is working on no check-in necessary so teams have autonomy over how they work and managers get the assurance they need to focus on the big picture it's such a challenge to get a team working together in a fluid way particularly when we're all working remotely these days in my experience monday.com does an amazing job of helping your organization stay in sync you can share ideas set goals and see how well you're meeting them with so much less friction monday.com is how effective teams meet their goals if you manage a team visit monday.com podcast for your free two-week trial it's the late 1970s in wise county texas just outside dallas george mitchell stands in a field of dusty dry grassland dotted with gas well derricks their scaffolding jutting into the sky he's the ceo and founder of mitchell energy and development and this is his natural gas field he runs his hand over his round bald head and glowers at the geologist standing next to him are you sure about this the geologist nods crossing his arms over his chest protectively mitchell has a reputation for having a volatile temper decades ago a secretary put up soundproofing material around the door to mitchell's office so visitors wouldn't hear his screaming now in his late 50s he's mellowed someone but this geologist has just delivered some devastating news i'm i'm afraid so i've run the numbers several times this field is drying up the wise county field is mitchell energy's key asset if it runs out of gas the company won't be able to meet its obligations it'll be as good as belly up mitchell resists the urge to kick the base of the nearest derrick how much longer have we got i'd say you've got 10 years give or take i guess we need to find another field then the geologist nods tentatively finding a new field won't be easy throughout the united states oil and gas production has been waning for years the country has become increasingly dependent on foreign oil mitchell looks around at the sprawling field that represents his life's work he remembers buying these three hundred thousand acres over twenty years ago now just two years ago they celebrated digging three thousand wells he loves the heavy equipment that marks the sight of each well the derricks like thousands of tiny eiffel towers dotting the plains of north texas he didn't bring this company into the world just to outlive it he's not going down without a fight the son of poor greek immigrants mitchell initially planned to be a doctor but he changed his mind after taking a summer job with his older brother in the louisiana oil fields the idea of studying maps and discovering hidden treasures excited him more than medicine after majoring in geology mitchell spent world war ii in the army corps of engineers when the war was over he convinced his brother to start wildcatting independently searching for oil and gas by studying maps of geological formations and guessing where reserves might be found it was a riskier path than taking a job with one of the major oil companies but something a professor said in college always stuck with him if you want to work for a big oil company fine you can drive around in a decent chevy but if you want to drive a cadillac you have to go out on your own mitchell wanted to drive a cadillac while the major companies and most wildcatters focused on oil mitchell and his brother realized there was less competition if they searched for natural gas at the time gas was the less profitable stepchild of the fossil fuel industry because oil is a liquid it's easier to transport and has a wider range of uses most importantly it can be converted into gasoline for powering cars and other vehicles natural gas on the other hand was limited to cooking and heating homes but mitchell predicted demand for gas would grow as more infrastructure was built to transport it in 1952 he bought cheap leasing rights to a field in wise county that the major companies had dismissed the rock and gas were too tightly packed together to be drilled by conventional means but mitchell had read an article about a relatively new technique called hydraulic fracturing it was expensive and the margins would be tight but mitchell was already seeing increased demand for natural gas in certain regions including chicago he decided to take a gamble and try this new technique his bet paid off in 1954 he scored a lucrative contract supplying 100 million cubic feet of natural gas to chicago per day for two and a half decades that gas came from his wise county field the same field he's just been told is running out of gas for mitchell natural gas is more than just a business in 1972 he read a book that argued the world would eventually run out of natural resources that got him interested in the environment he became an even stronger advocate for natural gas when he learned that it burned cleaner than coal gas's main competitor for heating homes his famous temper came out whenever he heard anyone say anything positive about coal for the rest of the late 1970s and into the early 80s mitchell searches for a new gas field his company explores potential fields in ohio california and new mexico but in every case the gas reserves are too deep and the rock too tightly packed to make extraction financially viable that book's hypothesis about the world running out of resources seems like it's coming true mitchell feels like there's a giant clock hanging over his head ticking down until his wise county field is drive and his company is out of business then he finds a potential lifeline it's 1981 and mitchell's in his office at the company's headquarters in houston the door creaks open and one of mitchell's senior geologist jim henry pokes his head in hey you wanted to see me yeah yeah come in have a seat i i just finished reading your paper about the barnett shale sitting underneath our wise county field it's very interesting stuff oh thank you sir you really think this barnet shale is full of natural gas under texas layers of rocks are stacked on top of each other like pancakes shale is one of the bottom pancakes eight thousand feet beneath the surface shale is a source rock for fossil fuels but gas and oil migrate upwards from the shale over time because of this most geologists have long assumed that very little fuel remained stored that deep but henry has found data suggesting otherwise i do think there's natural gas but i can't even begin to imagine how we'd extract it i hope this didn't give you the idea to why wouldn't i go for it we've been running around the country looking for gas and you're telling me we're already sitting on a heap of it we've used hydraulic fracturing on the layers above it why can't we use it on shale well shale is an incredibly tight compact type of rock you ever seen a piece of it it looks more like plastic than rock and the pores of shale are so small it's not like cracking a few pathways will free up much gas but mitchell's already left to his feet unable to contain his excitement well those are just problems to be solved sure but the solution may be more expensive than it's worth mitchell slams his hand down on the desk either we go out of business because we sat on our butts while our wells ran dry or we go out of business because we went out swinging mitchell takes a deep breath to calm himself this is why his secretary soundproofed his door he wipes his bald head and sits back down look at at least here we already own the leasing rights we have the infrastructure in place to drill we'd be crazy not to seize that advantage henry nods you're right let's try it mitchell leans forward a glint in his eye i don't want to find out on my deathbed that i was sitting on a fortune and someone else had the nerve to go after it this is ours jim let's beat the big boys to a breakthrough in the beginning fracking shale is less of a breakthrough and more of a lifeline for mitchell's company just as henry predicted it proves difficult to free significant amounts of gas from the fine-grained rock and fracking is expensive it takes millions of gallons of water and two hundred thousand dollars worth of specialized chemicals and lubricants to frack a single well the gas they're eeking out of the shale is prolonging the life of the field but it's not covering the costs of getting it out by 1992 mitchell energy has spent 35 million dollars fracking the barnett shale with little to show for it by 1995 the company's outlook is grim gas and oil prices always volatile are slumping the cost benefit of fracking shale becomes even less favorable and to take advantage of the lower market rates the city of chicago buys out its contract with mitchell two years before it's set to expire for the first time in 14 years mitchell is having doubts about ever getting enough gas from the shale to turn a profit it's 1996.\nmitchell is eating dinner with his wife and son todd who's in his late 30s and works as an executive in the company mitchell is uncharacteristically demoralized he's just gotten word that another shale will is underperforming and being shut down he's frustrated at its team's lack of progress i just don't understand why we can't make it work we know there's a heap of gas down there why can't we get it out todd looks up from his plate it's it's just the rock dad i mean you saw the reports it's over half quarts that's the fourth hardest mineral in the world of course we're having a hard time fracturing it todd leans across the table and puts his hand on his dad's arm look dad we really tried we drilled over 200 wells we've tried every kind of fracking material i mean heavy water oil propane ethane none of it frees enough gas to justify the cost i think we have to face the facts maybe there's a reason the big guys haven't tried this they have the best researchers in the business and they've decided you can't make money extracting gas from shale mitchell pulls his arm away abruptly what exactly is your suggestion if we don't make this work the company won't make it we're barely fulfilling our obligations as it is todd takes a deep breath i think we sell cut our losses while we can mitchell starts to protest but stops himself maybe todd is right mitchell's 77 he's also a real estate developer and owns a planned community north of houston it would be nice to focus on that rather than split his attention maybe his time in the energy industry has come to an end reluctantly mitchell nods all right todd's eyes go wide really yeah it's time a couple of months later mitchell is in his office going over the digital proofs for a prospectus his bankers put together to show potential buyers his secretary's at lunch so he grabs the phone himself mitchell here george it's uh patrick calling about that wise county well case it's mitchell's lawyer calling with an update on a long-running lawsuit in the 1980s a group of plaintiffs sued mitchell energy claiming that the company's drilling polluted their water wells it's the kind of lawsuit most fossil fuel companies get hit with all the time settling them or fighting them is part of the cost of doing business mitchell believes there's no merit to this one that the jury will rule in his favor he cradles the receiver against his shoulder and keeps scrolling through the prospectus only half listening to his lawyer so they finally came back with a verdict oh that damn time hopefully those greedy sobs learned their lesson about frivolous lawsuits how much in legal fees were we awarded um well that's the thing the jury sided with the plaintiffs mitchell's head snaps up his eyes bulging what they they had no facts just a bunch of sob stories and the damn jury fell for that crap what's the damage over 200 million dollars mitchell is stunned the whole company is worth less than 900 million this is a huge blow george george are you there mitchell looks at the prospectus open on his computer and fights the urge to punch the monitor trying to sell the company is pointless now no one is going to buy it carrying this kind of debt ratio mitchell feels the familiar anger boiling up in his gut this is a load of bull we're fighting this we will get this overturned but while he appeals the verdict mitchell needs to keep his company going and fracking jail seems like the only play he has left he has to figure out how to get large volumes of gas out of that stubborn rock the only question is how sleeping in your own bed pausing movies whenever you want talking to your dog all day like she's gonna talk back some things are just better at home and peloton delivers a workout experience that you'd never imagine was possible without ever leaving your place i mean this is fitness on your time at your pace whatever your mood or overall fitness goal you can mix and match classes choosing from cardio strength yoga pilates outdoor runs meditation and more i've been on the bike more than a few times and besides the truly incredible workouts the one thing i really enjoy is the curated music with epic artist collaborations and instructor curated playlists their music experience is one of the biggest motivators for me from keeping up with close friends to enjoying world-class workouts with people around the globe you'll never work out alone and with a boot camp between nap times or a ride before brunch you can seamlessly fit peloton into your life with the peloton bike there's nothing like working out from home learn more at onepeleton.com new members can try peloton classes free for 30 days at dot com slash app term supply that's o n e p e l o t o n dot com stop stop the well stop it's 1997.\nat a natural gas well on the wyze county shale field in texas an engineer named nick steinsberger frantically tries to get the attention of a well operator with thinning hair but a baby face steinsburger looks both older and younger than his 33 years two years ago george mitchell put him in charge of drilling the barnett's shale mitchells made it clear that the future of the company is resting on steinsberger's shoulders but now he's botched things stop the operator halts what's going on something's going wrong the chemicals aren't mixing right the fluid didn't thicken at all usually the water they pump into the wells is full of sand and chemical thickening agents so it has a texture like greeny jello but the liquid going into this well is still thin we need to stop sending it down the well and remix it the operator's face falls it's too late i already sent it all down steinsberger kicks the side of the rig damn it they both know the company's on thin ice the price of natural gas is down again every dollar counts and they've just wasted hundreds of thousands of them a few weeks later back at mitchell energy headquarters in houston steinberger taps his foot nervously as he waits to be called into the board room he's pretty sure he'll walk out of this meeting no longer a company employee but he has some cards up his sleeve that just might save his job there are no aces though they're closer to a pair of twos and exec sticks his head out nick come on in steinsberger takes a deep breath and walks into the boardroom to face his doom he stands at the head of the table mitchell is to his right mitchell's son todd on his left todd kicks the meeting off so what exactly happened out there it was just a mistake sir the mixing machine wasn't working properly and while we didn't catch it in time steinsburg shifts on his feet i i know i know this is an error that shouldn't have happened but something interesting did come out of it the executive stopped chatting and turned to look at him steinberger swallows and grits his teeth it's time to take his shot i checked the productivity of the well after we sent the unmixed liquid down it produced almost exactly as much gas as when we've used the thickened mixture most of the executives look at him confused unclear where he's going with this but mitchell gives him a small nod urging him on for the past 16 years we've been focused on getting more gas out of the wells what if instead we concentrated on reducing costs based on the results of this well it looks like we might not need as many pricey chemicals to frack shale an older engineer in the back snorts you got lucky son that well is a fluke there's no reason to think using water without chemicals will work long term well actually i talked to a friend in east texas they've seen success using fewer chemicals in their fracking operations the older engineer leans forward what kind of rock are they fracking um sandstone the engineer leans back in his seat well there you go sandstone is an entirely different beast it's brittle shale is clay it's essentially hardened mud what happens when you pour water on clay son steinsburger resists the urge to roll his eyes it soaks it up exactly you pour water on clay and it becomes less brittle it's a sponge that's the opposite of what we want you send water down that well you're going to trap the gas look i'm not saying there will be no chemicals just less less than one percent of the total fluid instead of the 10 percent we currently use if my calculations are correct it will drop the cost of a well from 300 000 to 85 000 mitchell who's been watching the exchange with an impassive look on his face holds up three fingers you can try it on three wells we'll see what the production numbers are and go from there in the back the old engineer shakes his head if this works i'll eat my degree steinsberger smiles relieved as the meeting breaks up mitchell claps steinsberger on the shoulder you have guts kid no one else would even try this if our industry had a gonads on the anvil award you'd win ah thank you sir of course if this doesn't work you're fired steinsburger nods as mitchell turns and ambles out of the boardroom his job is saved for now over the next several months steinsberger keeps checking his wells hoping against hope that they start producing more gas but the wells continue to crank out subpar amounts depressed he withdraws avoiding his family his wife starts researching other cities they can move to assuming it's just a matter of time before steinsberger loses his job but the next summer everything changes it's june 11 1998 just after dawn at a well in the wyze county field steinsberger steps out of his aging toyota camry sporting a safety vest and work boots a travel mug of coffee in his hand the sky glows lavender in the early morning light in front of him are approximately 35 trucks 20 or 18 wheelers carrying water tanks a dozen more are pump trucks that will force that water down the well and another few carry chemicals to make the water slippery and kill any bacteria about 40 men mill around taking care of the final tasks steinsberger approaches the foreman everything in place we're ready when you are steinsberger nods but the foreman hesitates you're sure about this new approach we have to try something new after his first three wells were disappointments steinsburger convinced his bosses to let him try with three more it was an even tougher cell his theory is that sending sand down with the water is clogging up the fractures instead of holding them open that's what's blocking the gas from escaping so today on their latest well named sh griffin number four they're trying a new idea using twice as much water as before and sending the sand down slower and later in the process it's a last-ditch effort steinsburger takes a deep breath let's do it steinsberger and the foreman enter the trailer that serves as the command center it's full of computers and monitors manned by workers controlling the pumps remotely from here steinsberger can monitor the pressure as workers send the water down the well if the pressure builds up and then falls it will tell him that fractures are being made once the fractures are big enough the pressure should drop quickly as the well swallows the water and sand in one big gulp steinsburger nods to the foreman who gives the command fire em up they send the first batch of water down the well steinsberger keeps his eye on the pressure gauge as it bounces up and down after an hour steinsberger taps the foreman on the shoulder okay send down the sand slowly the foreman nods and holds a walkie-talkie up to his mouth it's time for the sand as they pump over two million gallons of water mixed with chemicals and sand into the ground steinsberger watches for that precipitous drop in pressure the sign that the fracking has worked that the fractures are holding open for the next five hours he watches as the gauge bounces up and down finally it drops and keeps dropping the well is swallowing the water in one long gulp just like he wanted the foreman turns to him and grins broadly there we go steinsburger allows himself a small smile it's a good sign but they don't know if they hit their actual goal extracting approximately 800 000 cubic feet of gas per day and they won't know for several days first they have to remove the water they've pumped down there to allow the gas to rise up all steinsberger can do right now is wait and hope  five days later steinsburger is back on site he meets the foreman back in the command center the foreman is smiling wide water's almost all out gas is coming up take a look he indicates to a monitor that displays how much gas is flowing out of the well and into a pipeline steinsberger's eyes widen is this accurate calibrated it myself steinsburger doesn't say anything he's too overwhelmed let's not tell anyone just yet i want to track its output for a while usually fracked wells will start big and quickly taper off ultimately not netting much gas so before he says i told you so steinsberger wants to make sure this well keeps producing at a substantial level for the next three months he checks the well every day its production remains steady like a river he thinks flowing at a smooth and steady clip finally he's seen enough to share his results houston texas 1998.\nsteinberger is back in front of the mitchell executives sharing his success george mitchell and his son todd are in the front of the room steinsburger's nemesis the skeptical engineer is sitting in the back his arms crossed the well is churning out 1.5 million cubic feet of natural gas per day still that's nearly double what we projected mitchell smiles how is this happening steinberger can't help it he looks directly at the old engineer as he gives his answer there's much less clay in the barnett shale than we believed so when the water hits it at high enough force the shale shatters like it's glass our previous mixture was actually holding the shale together gradually sinking into the initial fractures and slowly clogging them up terrific you did it you cracked the mystery of the barnett shale mitchell turns and grins at the older engineer i hear diplomas taste great with hot sauce a geologist clears his throat he recently joined the company after working for chevron there's something else everyone turns to look at him you're wildly underestimating the amount of gas in the barnett shale what do you mean you based your estimates off the amount of gas in a shale field in the appalachians but the barnachille is deeper and under more pressure conditions that created more gas mitchell looks at the geologist his brow wrinkled we took measurements the geologist nods you used outdated methods when i was at chevron we used a new state-of-the-art technology to estimate the stores of gas in a section of the field just south of fort worth it's many times more than what you've estimated many mitchell narrows his eyes how much give it to me in square miles um does anyone have a calculator an engineer slides a calculator down the conference table to the geologist everyone watches while the geologist punches numbers into the machine and scribbles numbers on a pad finally he looks up there are 185 billion cubic feet of gas per square mile a huge smile spreads across mitchell's face 185 billion that's four times what we thought we had gentlemen this is huge the room erupts in celebration mitchell leans back in his chair and soaks it in he's always believed in the barnett's shale but it's been a rough few years there was the lawsuit scare though his appeal successfully reversed the decision and spared them 200 million but the price of their shares had dropped by over 50 percent in the past year he had to spin off his beloved real estate business to prop up the energy company he's recently been diagnosed with prostate cancer and his wife is showing early signs of alzheimer's but finally his stubbornness is paying off he was right he knows this could be one of the most significant advancements in the energy industry in decades and he's behind it the son of a greek goat herder but it will take years for steinsberger's slick water breakthrough to really transform the fossil fuel industry and when it does george mitchell won't be the one to exploit it it's summer 1999.\nwearing a hard hat and safety vest george mitchell leads representatives from various energy companies through his wise county gas field he and his wife are both grappling with health issues so mitchell has decided it's time to sell now that he knows the full potential value of this land he thinks he can fetch a small fortune for it but to do that he has to convince these energy reps that the barnett shale under his field is worth as much as his geologists say it is the summer sun beats down on them and mitchell wipes sweat from the back of his neck as you all know we've seen tremendous results with our slick water fracking technique and we have plenty of data showing that this field is rich in gas the representative from exxon mobil looks around mitchell's derricks clearly unconvinced the latest u.s geological survey estimates 10 trillion cubic feet of gas total i'm sure that's a lot to you mr mitchell but for us it's nothing to write home about their estimate is wrong our geologists have calculated a much larger amount the exon rep raises his eyebrows skeptically mitchell bristles he knows the major companies think he and his team are a bunch of yahoos with degrees from oklahoma state and texas tech not the ivy league pedigreed scientists that exxon and chevron employ but mitchell knows his ragtag team of geologists is right there's one rep on the tour who's not from a major oil company a guy from devon energy a small firm out of oklahoma they've built a reputation for having deep pockets and taking risks on unconventional energy assets maybe they have the vision to bet on his piece of the barnett shale mitchell turns to their rep and smiles as winningly as he can when you've seen our production numbers they're solid and i'm telling you there's way more gas in areas we haven't drilled in yet the devon representative nods rubbing the back of his neck you're doing real interesting work here there's no doubt about it but you know you've only fracked shale in a small section of the field who knows what the rest holds this is more potential than anything else and it's a hard time to take risks on potential natural gas prices have sunk to just two dollars and 30 cents per thousand cubic feet and no one's sure when they might rise again and the truth is mitchell hasn't actually proven that his field is as bountiful as he claims the devon rep looks at mitchell with sad eyes i really admire you mr mitchell everyone at devon does but i'm sorry i can't recommend buying this mitchell seethes all this time all this work and still no one believes what he's done it's april 2000 mitchell is on a speakerphone with the broker at goldman sachs who's been handling the sale of the company his son todd sits across from his father carefully studying his face which is getting redder and redder i'm sorry george uh we're out of options i i think you need to take the company off the market it's embarrassing to retract the sail it's admitting defeat and mitchell hates admitting defeat suddenly all the anger he's suppressed for the past nine months explodes he jumps up from his chair picks up his stapler from his desk and hurls it across the room denting the wall this is ridiculous any company with a dot-com at the end of its name is valued at millions of dollars doesn't matter what idiotic thing it sells slippers for your cat or umbrellas that turn inside out and my company which powers the whole goddamn internet i might add can't get a single measly offer what kind of backwards upside down world are we living in todd stands up alarmed dad please calm down you know it's not good for you to lose your temper like this for a moment the office is silent except for mitchell's ragged breathing then the broker who's still on speakerphone pipes up like i completely understand your frustration george it is a topsy-turvy world with this whole tech boom but this is just a setback you have a good year prove that your high production numbers weren't a fluke and we can shop the company around again fine mitchell picks up the receiver just so we can slam it down again he stares off into space as he continues to fume after a moment he focuses eyes on todd he's come up with a plan get as much acreage in the barn shale as possible todd's eyes widen are you sure mitchell nods sooner or later everyone's going to figure out what we've done and when they do they're going to want access to that shale field and in the meantime we're going to tie up as much of it as we can if we can't sell now then let's ensure a bigger sale later let's make them regret blowing us off over the next seven months mitchell energy expands its total holdings in the barnett shale to over 600 000 acres they reopen old wells they thought it'd run dry and start fracking them with their slick water techniques it works between the old wells and the new their production jumps tenfold it's one of the quickest production increases in energy history finally the industry takes notice including the head of devon energy larry nichols it's september 2000 fort worth texas larry nichols fans himself in the packed central room of the oil information library his neatly side-parted hair is damp with sweat he's here for a symposium on the barnett's shale since mitchell energy started extracting so much gas from shale it's become a hot topic over 200 oil executives engineers and geologists are on hand as nichols leafs through the pamphlet that's been passed out his jaw drops he knew mitchell was doing well but these numbers are higher than he ever imagined he's not the only one shocked the man next to him who works for a rival company scoffs this isn't possible these boys are juking their stats another rep agrees with him even if the data is true there's no way it'll last this is just another short-term wonder but nichols isn't so sure he gets up and works his way down the aisle excuse me pardon me in the lobby nichols calls the devon headquarters in oklahoma city at the other end of the phone an engineer named jeff hall answers hello jeff it's larry you evaluated mitchell energy last year correct yes yes sir fracking shale is an interesting idea but ultimately i'm not confident it can work out in the long term look into it again and do it fast hall goes over mitchell's numbers again this time with another year's data in the books the data is better than he expected especially on mitchell's oldest wells long after most wells would be running dry they're still going strong hall notices that although mitchell energy has leased a significant amount of acreage it's still only drilling on a small percentage of it he wonders what it would mean if they could duplicate mitchell's results across all of its acreage he does some quick math to extrapolate when he sees the number hall's eyes go wide immediately he calls nickels larry nichols here it's hall buy it nichols hangs up the phone a sly grin on his face in august 2001 devon energy announces it has bought mitchell energy for 3.1 billion dollars the techniques devon brings to drilling for natural gas combined with mitchell's slick water fracking will set off the next energy gold rush but in that rush a lot of ground will be shaken up literally everyday citizens will start to feel the effects of this once obscure technique and the environmental lobby will be forced to address fracking but not in a way anyone might expect from wondering this is episode 1 of fracking on american innovations a quick note about the recreations you've been hearing in most cases we can't know exactly what was said those scenes are dramatizations but they're based on historical research and if you'd like to learn more about fracking check out the frackers by gregory zuckerman american innovations is hosted by me steven johnson for more information on my books about science and innovation including my latest one extra life short history of living longer which is also a pbs series you can visit my website stevenberlinjohnson.com or you can follow me on twitter at stevenb johnson sound design on this episode is by eric rachelson this episode was written by austin rackless with editing by lizaville produced by emily frost our senior producer is andy herman our executive producers are jenny lauer beckman and marshall louis for wondering you",
