  "testcase": {
    "source_name": "coffeewars_1_A1_en_it",
    "source_text": [
      "Episode 01B:\nBusiness Wars: Coffee - Episode 1 Transcript\n[Music] \nIt's 1950 in Quincy, Massachusetts, at 6 a.m. on a cool September morning. A big, genial man named Bill Rosenberg opens his store's glass door. He calls the place Open Kettle. It's the first doughnut and coffee shop ever to provide seats for its customers. A group of men wait outside, eager for their morning java. Some are factory workers, mothers are salesmen and businessmen. The first to hurry in wears heavy pants and a work shirt.\n\"Worn in, Bell! Hey, Marty, ready for the usual? You bet! Coffee with two sugars and two glazed doughnuts. Got to have it to start my day!\"\nRosenberg turns to two men who sport suits, ties and hats. \"Morning, guys!\"\n\"Hey, Bill! Know what? Your coffee smells so good it wakes me up before I've had a sip!\"\nThe men sit at the low curved counter on leather-top stools. Rosenberg goes behind the counter. He smiles as he fills their cups with the drink they craved. As soon as the cups are empty, he fills them up again. Above the pot, a wall sign reads \"Ours is the best coffee in the world.\"\nEvery morning his shop fills up like this, and it never fails to make him smile. He heads into the kitchen. He loves watching the doughnut dough cook in the fryer's bubbling oil. When they turn golden brown, cooks whisk them from the fryer with giant spoons. He savors the donuts' rich, yeasty fragrance as they cool on metal racks. Beside the racks, deep bowls are filled with frosting in vanilla, chocolate, strawberry and maple flavors.\nRosenberg grabs a tablespoon and digs out a taste of strawberry icing\u2014his favorite. Dozens of donuts are iced, others are dipped in powdered sugar and shot full of cream. Many are stuffed with jelly in succulent flavors: lemon, blueberry, pineapple, apple spice. Rosenberg picks up a jelly doughnut. As he bites into it, a big magenta blob squirts onto his shirt. He laughs, wipes it off and licks it from his finger.\nThere's just one thing about his store that he doesn't like. He calls his staff together.\n\"We're doing great, but I hate the name of the store.\"\n\"You're the one who named it Open Kettle.\"\n\"True, true. I'll take the blame, but we need another name. Throw out anything that comes to mind.\"\n\"How about Mr. Donut?\"\n\"Oh, Best Donuts maybe... maybe. And I feel like we can do better. I got it! What do you do with a donut and coffee? You dunk the donut! That's it: Dunkin' Donuts!\"\n\"Yeah, it's got a nice ring to it!\"\nBut coffee is Rosenberg's true love. To prove it, the big new sign outside his store reads \"Dunkin' Donuts: The World's Finest Coffee.\"\nLike every dedicated coffee purveyor who will follow him, Bill Rosenberg is passionate about brewing the perfect cup. Decades in the future, that passion will take his company where he never imagined it would go: head-to-head with a global juggernaut called Starbucks.\n[Music] \nFrom Wondery, I'm David Brown, and this is Business Wars.\n[Music]\nWe live in a divided world. Are you a \"black, no sugar, two creams\" person, or do you go for \"grande, nonfat, no-whip, extra shot\"? Is your cup cardboard or compostable? Are you in and out in under a minute, or do you linger and hold meetings at a corner table? In other words, are you a Dunkin' loyalist or a Starbucks fan?\nThe answer may depend on where you grew up, how much you're willing to spend, and how much you embrace or eschew coffee's snobbery. In more and more places across the country and the world, you only have to cross the street to decide between the two brands.\nCoffee is a $100 billion-plus global industry. Starbucks and the company known for decades as Dunkin' Donuts rule the roost. But their battle is about even more than coffee. Ultimately, it's a culture clash of the everyman brand versus the elite one, and both companies want it exactly that way.\nThe seeds of their rivalry were planted in 1945. By then, the Second World War was over. The United States had become the most powerful country in the world. Everything about America seemed to be bigger and better than anywhere else. But there was one notable exception: America couldn't seem to make a really great cup of coffee. Coffee cognoscenti knew that American brands like Folgers and Maxwell House were as flavorful as pond water next to Italy's rich espresso or France's caf\u00e9 au lait.\nYet over the next decades, that would change. It would take a former eighth-grade dropout named Bill Rosenberg to teach average Joes to take their cup of joe seriously.\nThis is Episode One: A Steamy Culture Clash.\nIt's May 1945. Bill Rosenberg is standing outside the offices of the La Touraine Coffee Company in downtown Boston. Men and women are rushing by him on their way to work, but Rosenberg gazes up at the building in a daze. He's about to plunk down more money than he's ever paid for anything in his life. He's scared but excited.\nHis food truck business is booming. What started as one truck serving hungry factory workers has expanded to 240 trucks. Sixty percent of his sales are coming from coffee\u2014it's his most profitable item by far. But Rosenberg's not satisfied with the coffee he's serving. He's determined to find the very best product for his customers.\nHe enters La Touraine's offices. A waiting salesman motions for him to take a seat. Rosenberg removes his hat and sits down. Not one for small talk, he gets right to business. He's there to buy coffee for his booming food truck business. The salesman's eyes light up.\n\"Look, Mr. Rosenberg, I'd hate to talk you out of a sale, but are you sure about your order? It's enormous!\"\n\"Believe me, I need it all.\"\n\"How much are you gonna sell the coffee for?\"\n\"Ten cents a cup.\"\nThe salesman rolls his eyes. \"Are you crazy? You can't sell coffee in the Boston area for a dime! All the cafeterias sell this for a nickel!\"\n\"True, but you can't sell good coffee for a nickel. I know you can't, but that's what people are paying. Those other outfits are selling six ounces of flavored water. I'll give people twelve ounces of real coffee for a dime.\"\n\"I'm telling you, they won't pay it.\"\nRosenberg smiles. He stands and puts on his hat. \"Just watch me.\"\nRosenberg doesn't make coffee the usual way by percolating it in a metal pot. He's convinced that percolated coffee loses flavor because too much water flows through the grounds. So he makes drip coffee in 100-gallon stainless steel containers. A huge cloth bag containing dozens of pounds of ground coffee sits on top. After 100 gallons of water are boiled, they're poured over the grounds just until the brew reaches the right strength.\nCustomers can tell the difference between this coffee and other kinds they've had, and Rosenberg's right\u2014they will pay for quality, even at ten cents a cup. He can't keep up with the demand. Soon he has five 100-gallon containers with timers that regulate the flow of the water. A conveyor belt moves the brewed coffee along in ten-pound tanks. Some get creamed, others get sugar, others get cream and sugar. Then they're labeled and sent out on his trucks.\nRosenberg is pleased with his luncheon service, but he's ambitious and restless. He's always been a natural entrepreneur. As a boy during the Great Depression, he hauled a big block of ice to the racetrack on a very hot day, then he sold chips of ice for a dime apiece and made $17.\nAt home in Quincy, Massachusetts, he sits down to dinner with his wife, Bookie. He's quiet, and that's unnatural for Rosenberg.\n\"Bill, you've hardly said a word. What's on your mind?\"\n\"I know I should be content\u2014business is booming\u2014but every day I'm seeing more and more donut shops opening.\"\n\"Oh, come on, Bill. You can't make money selling coffee and donuts.\"\n\"A cup of coffee and a donut goes for fifty cents, but the key is volume. The owner of Cottage Donuts is building a huge new home a few towns over, and all he owns are two small donut stores. That tells me there's tremendous opportunity in this business.\"\n\"Look, I know you love to eat donuts, but that doesn't mean you should be selling them.\"\n\"But I'll lead with my coffee\u2014people love it\u2014then I'll add donuts. That's got to be a winner. Trust me on this, Bookie.\"\nMemorial Day, 1948. Bill Rosenberg stands outside his coffee and donut store, a giant pair of scissors in his hand. A curious crowd is gathering.\n\"What's going on? Why are those orange flags flying over your store?\"\n\"It's our opening day\u2014a big occasion! Hang around, we'll be giving out free donuts and coffee.\"\n\"I can smell them from here! They sure smell good!\"\nA thick red ribbon spans the shop's door. Rosenberg beams as he cuts it. The crowd applauds and hurries inside for their free doughnuts and java.\nWithin months, this small store is taking in $3,000 a week\u2014that's equivalent to $31,000 today. Coffee accounts for 60% of sales. Customers don't seem to mind the price. In addition to coffee, Rosenberg offers 52 revolving flavors of donuts for five cents each.\nIn 1949, the store celebrates its first anniversary. It's a momentous occasion, but Rosenberg is hungry for more. He has a dream of opening stores all over Massachusetts, and that's what he does, starting along Route 1 north of Boston. Only he makes his shops into living theaters.\nEach store has a window that lets customers peer into the kitchen. They watch bakers roll out the dough, dust it with flour and cut the doughnuts. Then they put them on a screen, lower the screen into the hot oil and fry them into delicious morsels. The visual, combined with the enticing scent, is irresistible.\nBy 1955, five years after the first store opened, the demand keeps growing. Rosenberg can't keep up. He calls his staff together. There's a new business model\u2014he's heard it called franchising. The staff looks puzzled.\n\"I've heard of it, but it's not popular. People say it's a misfit for business.\"\n\"Well, to me it's brilliant! If we franchise, we'll sell the rights to our name, logo, products and business model to a third party. That makes us the franchisor, they'll be the franchisee. They put up the money for the business, we charge an initial franchise fee, take a 5% cut of their sales, plus 5% annually for advertising. In exchange, we provide all the guidance they'll need to make their own Dunkin' Donuts store successful.\"\n\"But why would we tell them our trade secrets?\"\n\"Because when you share with others, everyone benefits. They do well, we all do well. Believe me, it'll be a cash cow.\"\nIn 1955, the first Dunkin' Donuts franchise opens in Dedham, Massachusetts, about 20 miles from Boston. In the next ten years, the number of franchise shops grows to 100.\nBut even though business is booming, Bill Rosenberg is uneasy. The eighth-grade dropout doesn't feel confident about taking Dunkin' Donuts to the next stage. He wants the company to grow exponentially. That will mean hiring large swaths of people, understanding management decisions at a higher level, and even preparing to go public one day\u2014skills he's not sure he has.\nSo in 1963, Bill hands the CEO post to his 25-year-old son Bob. He's just graduated from Harvard Business School and looks like a younger but speckled version of his enthusiastic father. Bill stays on as chairman emeritus, but while he's formally ceded power to his son, he's not about to sit back and let Bob run the show.\nOn one of his son's first days as CEO, Bill takes Bob aside.\n\"Bob, I just got a cup of coffee in the office and had to toss it out. What happened to our brewing standards?\"\n\"Well, you know, I don't drink coffee at all.\"\n\"Well, you better drink it! There's nothing more delicious!\"\nBill is convinced Bob is the right person to lead the company. He's smart, he's educated\u2014after all, he's got a fancy MBA from Harvard. Trouble is, Bob isn't so confident. Being CEO is the first job he's ever had.\nStill, during his first years, the company does well. When they go public in 1968, Bob proudly tells his father, \"We're the darlings of Wall Street!\"\nBut Bob is moving beyond Dunkin' Donuts' New England roots and growing the company too fast. When they move into Texas, the result is disastrous. Six of the ten new stores soon close. The franchisee angrily blames the corporate office for not advertising enough and not paying enough attention to their stores. The company is bleeding money.\nWhen outraged franchisees sue Dunkin' Donuts, alleging the company violated its contract by focusing on selling new franchises rather than helping the ones they already own, Bob hears about the suit and is so upset he vomits. Now he has to tell his father.\n\"What are the franchisees threatening?\"\n\"Their suit is ridiculous! Not only is what they're alleging not true, they're demanding four times the value of the entire company!\"\n\"Well, you better do something fast, because if they win that suit, we're finished!\"\nBob Rosenberg is summoned to a meeting of the board of directors. They seem to be avoiding his gaze.\n\"We're sorry to say this, but it's time for you to resign.\"\n\"Look, just give me one more quarter, please! I think we can right this ship!\"\nNew Haven, Connecticut, 1972. Edna DeMary is standing in the kitchen of the Dunkin' Donuts her husband franchises. She often comes in to watch the doughnuts get made\u2014her own form of quality control. She watches the baker cut the centers out of the raw donut dough before he fries it. But instead of letting him throw out the doughy center, she takes one and tosses it in the fry-o-lator.\nIt's delicious and small\u2014a little treat that she gives to employees. Then the store starts selling them. And when Edna rolls the donut holes in coconut, coats them with chocolate, or fills them with cream or jelly, and they sell a lot, the DeMary's know they've got a hit on their hands.\nThey summon Bob Rosenberg. As Rosenberg enters the New Haven store, he sees mounds and mounds of doughnut holes on gold trays. He pops one in his mouth.\n\"Mmm-hmm, these are amazing!\"\n\"Customers love them, kids love them! Eight to ten percent of our sales are coming from doughnut holes. We should be selling these in all of our franchises, but we need a name for them\u2014something cute, catchy.\"\n\"I'll run it by our advertising agency.\"\nThe advertisers draw inspiration from a movie sensation, The Wizard of Oz. These tasty doughnut holes become known as Munchkins.\nOn the day that Munchkins are launched, Bob Rosenberg goes to Dunkin' Donuts' original store in Quincy, Massachusetts. Kids race to the trays of Munchkins. Their parents buy them a dozen of the tiny treats, then they buy donuts and coffee for themselves. By noon, the store is packed.\nThe franchisees do great business with Munchkins. Their sales grow, so does their confidence in Dunkin' Donuts management. They drop the lawsuit. Bob Rosenberg keeps his job. Munchkins saved the day.\nBut there's another threat looming on the horizon, 3,100 miles away on the West Coast. There's a visionary from the Netherlands named Alfred Peet who's trying to teach Americans that coffee comes from roasted beans, not just a can of grounds. It's a lesson that will change the lives of two young Americans. When it does, the world of coffee will be transformed, and Dunkin' will find itself in a rivalry that threatens to boil over.\n[Music]\nThanks to Avalara for sponsoring our show. You know, nobody goes into business because they want to collect sales tax\u2014or if they do, that's a person I want to meet because I have a lot of questions! And no, collecting sales tax and other transactional taxes for the government is just something that businesses of all sizes have to do, right? Well, now there are more than 12,000 tax jurisdictions in the US alone. I want you to think about that: 12,000! You combine that with thousands of product taxability rules and what you have, right? A massive headache for businesses trying to figure it all out.\nThat is where Avalara can help you. Avalara is tax compliance done right. Their software automatically calculates the right amount of tax that should be charged for every product in every transaction\u2014all in the blink of an eye. Plus, Avalara files all of the sales tax returns wherever and whenever they are due, and manages the piles of documentation digitally.\nIsn't it time to stop the endless headaches that come with trying to figure this all out on your own? You bet it is! It's time to get Avalara. Find out more at avalara.com/BW. That's Avalara\u2014A-V-A-L-A-R-A.com/BW.\n[Music]\nIt's 1967, summertime in Berkeley, California. Two 30-something men dressed in t-shirts and jeans are sitting at a table in the newly opened Pete's Coffee and Tea. A few years ago they were college roommates. Now they're both married, living in Seattle. Jerry Baldwin is an English teacher, Gordon Bowker is a writer. After taking a day at a literary conference, they need a jolt of caffeine.\nBaldwin takes a sip from the cup in front of him. \"Wow, this is like nothing I've ever tasted before!\"\nBowker tries the coffee. \"It's fantastic! That flavor just fills your mouth. This isn't something you drink just for the jolt\u2014it's ambrosia! It's like fine wine!\"\n\"Well, I'll tell you one thing: I'm never drinking anything else!\"\nHe means it. From then on, Bowker and Baldwin have bags of Peet's being shipped to their homes in Seattle. The more they drink it, the more they love it. They're curious about the shop's founder, Alfred Peet.\nCoffee is in Alfred Peet's DNA. His father was a Dutch trader importing beans from Indonesia, East Africa and the Caribbean. They arrange a meeting with him in Berkeley.\nBowker and Baldwin sink into Peet's comfortable chairs in his small office. Peet is 47 and has a gentle manner and bright, piercing eyes. Peet senses he has an eager audience and tells them his story.\n\"In Amsterdam, as a young man, I worked for a coffee importer. I traveled to coffee plantations in Java and Sumatra. I had learned about varieties of beans and what makes a great cup.\"\n\"When did you come to America?\"\n\"In 1955. The morning after I arrived in San Francisco, I went out for breakfast. I ordered a cup of coffee and it tasted like dishwater! I spit it out. I couldn't find good coffee anywhere. I asked myself, 'How is this possible in the world's richest country?' So for the last ten years I've been importing good coffee beans to the States, and last year I opened my store.\"\n\"And what makes your coffee taste so amazing?\"\n\"I roast my beans until they turn dark\u2014that's the European way. It brings out their full flavor. And I blend beans from different countries. I analyze each bag of beans to determine a roast suited to its particular characteristics.\"\n\"This is fascinating! I never knew so much went into it!\"\nOver the next three years, Baldwin and Bowker keep ordering Peet's coffee beans to be shipped to their homes in Seattle, but they never seem to have enough. Then in the summer of 1970, Bowker has an epiphany. He rushes over to Baldwin's home and bursts through the door.\n\"Let's open a coffee store here in Seattle! Import our own beans and sell them\u2014do what Peet does!\"\n\"Whoa, wait, wait, wait... really? You're serious?\"\n\"Yes! Listen, we're addicted to those great beans. Why not get the rest of Seattle addicted to them too?\"\nWell, Baldwin loves the idea. They each invest $1,350 and borrow another $5,000 from a local bank. Bowker and Baldwin will run operations. They meet at Bowker's home, pour themselves some strong coffee\u2014of course\u2014and hammer out the details.\nBaldwin's stuck on the most pressing first detail: the name.\n\"Someone once told me that words beginning with the letter S are especially powerful.\"\n\"Well, there's an old mining town called Starbo. We can name it after that.\"\n\"Wait, wait, wait! Remember the name of the first mate in Moby-Dick? Starbuck! That's it\u2014with a twist: we'll call it Starbucks!\"\nNow Baldwin and Bowker need a logo. They pour over pictures in old marine books. One of the images is of a 16th-century Norse woodcut. She's a two-tailed mermaid. Her image is as seductive as the coffee itself.\nOn March 31st, 1971, Starbucks Coffee, Tea and Spice opens in a small store in Seattle's historic Pike Place Market. The store is designed to look slightly nautical. A long wall with wooden shelves displays 30 different kinds of coffee. They sell only coffee beans and the best home coffee machines, but they sometimes offer samples served in porcelain cups that make the coffee taste even better.\nThey buy their coffee beans from Alfred Peet. Once the store is open, each of the founders travels to Berkeley where Alfred Peet teaches them the art of roasting beans.\nBut from the start, they're bucking the odds. Seattle is in an economic downturn\u2014it's called the Boeing Bust. Boeing is the city's biggest employer and they've just fired 62,000 workers. Yet Starbucks catches on. It's a hit with Seattle citizens who love the idea of savoring their coffee at home, especially on those gloomy days in winter. And Starbucks is the only place in Seattle that offers quality coffee.\nBy 1981, Bowker and Baldwin have four stores in the Seattle area. It's not a lot, but it's enough to seize the attention of the man who will view coffee as a kind of religion. He's 28 years old and his name is Howard Schultz. From the moment he encounters Starbucks, he and the entire business of coffee will never be the same.\nOn the next episode: Dunkin' Donuts hits it out of the park with an overworked baker, and Howard Schultz's trip to Italy leaves him forever changed.\n"
    ],
    "source_config": "en-US,C2,,",
    "target_config": "it-IT,A1,,"
  },
