      "it is fall 1972.\nin an office in romania a phone is ringing it's an office that belongs to the agent of a tennis player named elie nastase nasty for short starting in the 1960s nasty clawed his way to the top of pro tennis beating out the likes of the american great stan smith and doing it with his trademark arrogance nasty is cocky and crude but despite all that he's also one of the most popular athletes in the world like a lot of european players nasty used to wear apparel made by adidas the german-based sneaker giant but more recently he's taken to sporting a pair of shoes called match points which are made by a young company out of oregon in the usa which as it turns out is the purpose of this particular call hello the caller in his brisk american accent identifies himself as a one-time runner named phil knight founder of along with his former track coach bill bowerman a portland company called nike nike is only a few years old but already by focusing on technical improvements nike has made a big splash on the growing sneaker market a market that includes among others new balance deodorant converse reebok and what knight likes to call the biggest monster out there you know the one adidas has deep roots in europe and they've succeeded in signing dozens of the world's most popular athletes to endorsement deals deals that ensure adidas remains in front of as many eyeballs as possible knight understands that if he's going to compete globally with adidas he's going to need at least a few high-profile athletes endorsing nike even if they are you might say nasty and nasty the man is a good bet since he already wears nikes knight wants to seal this special relationship by paying him enough not to give so much as a thought to lacing up a pair of adidas shoes over the phone he suggested nasty's agent that he'd be willing to pay the tennis player five thousand dollars for his exclusive endorsement he breaks into a cold sweat as he makes the offer truth be told he doesn't exactly have five grand laying around how about fifteen thousand ten thousand it's absolutely as high as i can go hmm deal a few days later knight flies to omaha where nasty is competing in a tournament over dinner with nasty and his wife who's a model knight presents nasty with the papers and he signs them one bottle of wine is consumed and then another that evening knight returns to his hotel room more than a little tipsy he feels grand wouldn't you king maker a tycoon which brings to mind another feeling almost instantly thanks to nasty and his agent there's a feeling of lightness in his wallet but as queasy as he might be in the wee small hours of inebriated reflection knight has no idea how much worse he could be feeling right now if only he knew what adidas was up to right now well as it happens the german firm has been collecting hundreds of endorsements hey i want you to think about something for me when was the last time that dinner was more than just a meal for you you know when was the last time that dinner was an opportunity to spend some quality time with the people you love to cook and eat with be honest well for me it was this week and i did it with plated plated sources artisanal items and high quality ingredients including sustainably raised meats and organic produce wherever possible and they deliver everything you need to cook in just the right portions right to your door and there are 20 chef designed recipes to choose from every single week i was so impressed by the quality of the delivery and my wife was blown away by the step-by-step instructions the whole family was knocked out by the flavor we dim the lights a bit got some music going on the speaker it was a first class dining experience right there at home and if you've got younger kids like us well i'd recommend the beef bolognese or the lamb hero both are wholesome they're fun to prepare as a family the food's satisfying with sublime flavors so it's grown up food and the portions are perfect everyone looks forward to the next delivery we know the evening is going to be special when our box from plated arrives at the door now it's your turn discover your ideal dinner experience go to plated.com bw to get 25 percent off your first four weeks for a limited time only terms apply see plated.com bw for details that's plated.com bw from wondering this is business wars i'm david brown today we're exploring the multi-million dollar rivalry between the two biggest sneaker manufacturers on earth nike and adidas this is episode 3 walking billboards in the last episode we looked at the origins of these two corporations born on separate sides of the atlantic in very different eras and run by very different kinds of executives today we're going to fast forward a bit to the 1970s and the first real collision between the two which took place not coincidentally at the dawn of the era of big ticket endorsements by top athletes of course as long as there have been professional sports they've been professional athletes willing to accept money to wear certain clothes or attach their fame to other brand names consider the case of hunus wagner the early 20th century baseball player who famously allowed his likeness to be affixed to cigars and razor blade advertisements in 1905 wagner was paid to endorse a certain bat company you may have heard of louisville slugger but the deal wagner in louisville struck was a little more than nominal compared to the endorsement machinery that comes online starting in the 1960s across the globe television networks are signing syndication deals with various sports leagues in europe it's rugby and soccer in the us it's major league baseball the national football league in the national hockey league in january of 1968 a now-defunct syndication network called tvs broadcast the first nationally viewed college basketball game it's an exhilarating match between the ucla bruins and the houston cougars it goes down in history as the game of the century paid attendance tonight 52 693 the largest paid audience to ever see basketball anywhere at any level three seconds the game is so dramatic that it is broadcast daily around the country for the next week and the number of fans explodes so do the number of broadcasts of college games it's a revelation for the entertainment industry and for americans who can watch their favorite athletes compete too in real time almost any day of the week now fans can just flick on the television set to watch their favorite teams tracking players on basketball courts football fields and baseball diamonds of course athletic companies see something else here the world's largest billboards not just on the walls of the stands but running passing dribbling batting kicking three-dimensional ambassadors of all things in athletic wear a marketing revolution is about to take hold and it's the dassler family that first leverages these developments on a mass scale when we last checked in with the dastards rudy and adi had parted ways with rudy starting puma and adi founding adidas by the 1960s adi is still an active presence at adidas headquarters in germany but born at the turn of the century well he's getting on in years he's taking more of a back seat at his own company leaving most of the day-to-day decisions to his 30-something son horst in temperament horst is less like his father than his estranged uncle rudy he's happier cutting deals than he is at the drafting table his motto business is about relationships decades on horst remains famous for his business acumen he understands that it's all fine and good to make a nice shoe but in the television age with millions of people around the world having color tvs parked in their living rooms well if your shoe isn't on that box you might as well not bother making it at all he goes hard after contracts with top athletes often competing directly with puma which is now controlled by rudy dassler's son and horst's cousin armen and horst is not above playing rough either 1968 the mexico city summer olympics soon will be underway and horst and armen have been doling out cash to athletes in various disciplines they don't ask much in exchange only that the athletes wear the right shoes when they compete in advance of the games horse travels to the olympic trials near lake tahoe in california there he happens to note that a top us runner lee evans is wearing puma sneakers known as brush shoes which have dozens of tiny pins on the soles to improve traction when evans completes a record-setting 400-meter dash horse starts muttering aloud to everyone with an earshot those pins they're not that different from spikes are they and don't olympic track regulations forbid athletes from competing with more than six spikes on each soul well that can't be fair horse sits back and waits for what he knows will happen a game of telephone ensues and soon horst's objections have reached the ears of olympic officials who decide that evans record is null and void not exactly a good look for one of puma's most visible celebrity endorsers puma is left flat-footed still it presses forward by the late 1960s it has become apparent to executives at both puma and adidas that if the companies are to continue to grow they will need to expand further into the united states and particularly into the fastest growing sports professional basketball and football fresh off the olympic trials embarrassment puma strikes first paying a young quarterback named joe namath 25 thousand dollars to wear its sneakers on the gridiron nbc sports presents the third afl nfl world championship game the super bowl when namath's new york jets beat out the baltimore colts to win the 1969 super bowl the man who'd be celebrated as broadway joe is sporting those puma shoes namath the snell now barrels bobby boyd over and then is dropped at the point payoff is immediate and big 400 000 pairs of nameth branded pumas are sold that year instead of attempting to follow puma into the football arena adidas fires back with the release of a basketball sneaker called the superstar before the superstar the vast majority of professional basketball players wore chuck taylor all-stars you know floppy canvas shoes made by a massachusetts company called converse but horst and his designers determined that the all-stars leave the ankles too prone to twists and strains they also figured that players would be better served by stronger toe protection working out of a lab in france where adidas had recently expanded the company devises the first run of superstars with their iconic shell toe triple stripe design and thick cuff that encircles the ankle and soon most of the members of the boston celtics winners of the 1969 basketball championships are wearing those superstars by the early 1970s approximately 85 percent of pro basketball players hit the courts in adidas shoes some are being paid a lot of money to do it example a kareem abdul-jabbar a seven foot two-inch los angeles lakers star who is signed to a deal rumored at 25 000 per year kareem is the face of a new generation of basketball talent and he becomes the first nba player to get his own custom sneaker it's dubbed the adidas jabar when kareem tears up the court in front of millions of viewers it's the three stripes the fans are seeing still no company can hang on to market control forever and soon a new challenger is rising out of the west coast and unlike adidas a big european corporation with only a small permanent footprint in the u.s this newcomer is american to the core there's a company that i've been hearing about more and more in recent weeks it's called zip recruiter have you heard of zip recruiter are you hiring well every business needs great people and a better way to find them i guess that's why we keep hearing about zip recruiter because it's so hard to find the right person these days the job market is so tight you gotta find something better than just posting your job online and hoping that the right people are going to see it ziprecruiter goes that extra mile they learn what you're looking for they identify people with the right experience and invite them to apply to your job in fact eighty percent of employers who post a job on zip recruiter get a quality candidate through the site in just one day if you're hiring believe me the right candidates are out there zip recruiter is how you find them right now our listeners can try ziprecruiter for free that's right free just go to ziprecruiter.com bw that's ziprecruiter.com bw ziprecruiter.com bw zip recruiter the smartest way to hire you know i think a lot of us thought that losing your hair was an old man's thing but science tells us that by age 35 two out of three men will start losing their hair hair loss is actually easy to prevent if you get started early i want to tell you about keeps it's a new company offering a simple clinically proven and affordable way to stop hair loss with keeps it's easier than ever for guys like you to keep what's on top keeps offers the only two fda approved hair loss products clinically proven to keep the hair you have you can sign up in less than five minutes it's all done online you answer a few questions you snap some photos a licensed doctor remotely reviews your information and adjusts the prescription just for you all without you ever having to leave your couch and within two to three days a three-month supply of your treatment will arrive discreetly packaged at your door what are you waiting for stop hair loss today the easy way with keeps offering customized treatment plans with the only fda approved hair loss products for about a dollar a day from the comfort of your couch to receive your first month of treatment for free go to keeps.com bw that's keeps.com slash bw that's a free month of treatment at keeps.com slash bw keeps hair today hair tomorrow you gotta love that 1970 hayward field home of the university of oregon's track squad a lean and angular runner races around the track his legs gobbling up the meters his name is steve prefontaine pre for short and he's destined to become one of the most famous runners in history nearby stands his coach bill bowerman bauer sees something very special in pre he always wanted to race out front from the start like he was trying to get away from something just where and when this compulsion came from no one can say for sure pre and bowerman grow closer and when bowerman joins phil knight to start blue ribbon which would later become nike he asks pre to wear his shoes pre agrees why not these shoes are good in the summer of 1972 presets the american record in the 5 000 meters at an olympic qualifying event but in the actual olympic race he narrowly misses the podium all together not even a bronze he's furious at himself phil knight tries to lift his spirits hey the city officials in eugene did you know they're naming a street after you great what are they going to call at 4th street but soon with knight and bowerman's encouragement pre is back on the track setting records again there's a problem though the runners broke you can't go around with a begging bull we'll get you a job where let's not worry about that part right now soon pre is working between races and training out of a nike storefront in oregon his annual salary is 5k a year he gets a business card that reads national director of public affairs the reasons why some brands fail and others soar are many of course sometimes it's the result of determination and sometimes is the result of something close to luck you know being in the right place at the right time or finding the right person at the right time for nike which has a fraction of the market cloud of adidas in the early 1970s steve prefontaine is that person not just an athlete but charismatic handsome outgoing remembering those years phil knight once said that pre preached nike as a gospel and brought thousands of new people into our revival tent he is a symbol of rebellion and iconoclasm every new race he goes to every athlete he meets pre-makes sure to highlight the benefits of those new sneakers by that small company back in oregon brand recognition grows of course and so do sales thousands of pairs of waffle racers disappear from shelves but now knight has a dilemma a choice you might say he can position nike as a running shoe company or he can go big time expand into other sports and try to go head-to-head with adidas and puma and converse on their own turf knight decides to play long ball and take on the competition in september of 1971 knight flies back to japan the country where nike's precursor blue ribbon sports had been born there he places orders with a manufacturer called nisho for several new styles of sneakers the sample versions of the shoes are finished a few days later on a sunday there's no one in the knee show offices but it doesn't matter knight has a key so he heads out to the company's headquarters on his own and lets himself into the lobby for hours he stares at the shoes that will make nike or break it some of these sneakers like the cortez racer already have names but others don't and over the course of the day shuttered away in the empty office knight embarks on a naming spree removing each pair from its box and holding it up to the light he names one pair of white leather tennis sneakers the wimbledon and another the forest hills after the location of the first u.s open he names one of the running shoes the marathon and for his biggest gamble a mid-top basketball shoe that will compete directly against the adidas superstar he goes with blazer a nod to his hometown nba team the portland trailblazers after sunset and exhausted knight ducks back outside in the neon darkness tokyo streets are buzzing with activity and phil knight's head is buzzing too it's a transformative moment and he knows it he feels spent but he feels proud i felt everything i ever hoped to feel after a day's work i felt like an artist a creator i looked back over my shoulder took one last look at the knee show offices under his breath knight mutters we made this it takes some time for adidas to feel the heat from its newest competitor but when it does it acts quickly in the run-up to the 20th olympiad in munich just a few months before phil knight makes his pitch to a tennis player named nasty horse dumps thousands of dollars into paying and outfitting an estimated 80 percent of the athletes at the games in adidas apparel from shoes to tracksuits and then the master stroke do you remember mark spitz he was kind of like the michael phelps of the 1970s handsome american swimmer dazzling smile a disco mustache his chest covered in gold medals he was an athlete at the absolute pinnacle of his sport well after spitz nets his first gold in the 1972 games horst makes him a proposal look we'd really like you to wear our shoes when you climb up there on the podium it's a great opportunity that's not gonna work you know those warm-up pants we wear when we get out of the pool no one's gonna be able to see our shoes okay i've got an idea it's turns for home mathis is to his left height and reich is to his right the next time spitz gets up on a podium he's carrying a pair of adidas gazelle sneakers in his hands and when he leaves the pool area he's carrying those shoes with him too making sure the cameras catch every stitch the olympic committee is furious how could spitz and dassler turn a hallowed sporting ground into a fashion runway but destler is unapologetic he's a businessman he's made a smart business move by the mid 1970s adidas is selling more than 150 kinds of shoes and has expanded heavily into the larger world of sports apparel at this point adidas is raking in an incredible 500 million dollars in revenue a year but as in the world of sports no company is dominant forever phil knight and nike are about to take flight in our next episode of nike versus adidas phil knight makes his move and a young player named michael jordan gives the americans a very big boost i hope you enjoyed this episode of business wars please subscribe on apple podcast stitcher iheartradio or wherever you're listening right now you'll find a link on the episode notes just tap or swipe over the cover art you'll also see some offers from our sponsors and we hope you'll support our show by supporting them and if you like what you heard well we'd love you to give us a five star rating and tell your friends how to subscribe too another way to support us is to answer a short survey at wundery.com survey and tell us what business war stories you'd like to hear while you're at it i'm your host david brown matthew cher wrote this story karen lowe is our senior producer and editor sound designed by bay area sound our executive producers are marshall louis and hernan lopez for wondering\n"
