{
  "testcase": {
    "source_name": "b298ca91-8dbd-49a1-ad00-10795c3d19b5",
    "source_text": [
      "How I Reduced My React Native App Bundle Size by 70% \ud83d\ude80 When migrating my app from React Native CLI to Expo, I configured and performed an update via EAS.\nI noticed that the update file size was quite large, so I started investigating.\nInitially, I couldn't run , as I had to make some adjustments to Babel.\nOnce I managed to get it working, here\u2019s what I found.\nThe first step was to understand what was making the bundle so large.\nTo do this, I used , which allows visualizing the modules and dependencies that make up the bundle: This helped me identify unnecessary packages and heavy dependencies, here is the result: One of the biggest surprises was that Font Awesome was taking up a significant amount of space(6.39 MB).\nUpon further investigation, I realized that due to the way imports were structured, all icons were being included in the bundle, even though I was only using a small subset.\nInstead of using this: This ensures that only that specific icon will be added to the bundle, allowing tree-shaking to remove unused icons.\nAfter making these import changes, I ran again, and the results were impressive: 4.\nWhere Else Could I Reduce More?\nAs I continued analyzing, I noticed a package called .\nI had no idea what it was or why it was included in the project.\nAfter digging deeper, I discovered that this package had been deprecated for over six years.\nI then checked which package was still using it and found that depended on To resolve this, I looked into the library\u2019s GitHub repository and found that in newer versions, this dependency had been removed.\nSo, I updated to the latest version in my project.\nAfter implementing these changes, I ran again, and the final results were: I achieved a total reduction of ~70% in my React Native app\u2019s bundle size.\nAfter applying these optimizations, my app\u2019s bundle size was reduced by 70%, resulting in a lighter and more efficient application.\nThis improvement not only positively impacted the user experience but also reduced download time and storage usage on the device.\nIf you are also facing issues with bundle size, I recommend checking your imports and dependencies to identify unnecessary bloat!\n"
    ],
    "source_config": "en-US,C2,,",
    "target_config": "fr-FR,B2,,"
  },
  "testconf": {},
  "history": [
    {
      "operator": "request content",
      "source_name": "b298ca91-8dbd-49a1-ad00-10795c3d19b5",
      "segment_num": 0,
      "source_text": "How I Reduced My React Native App Bundle Size by 70% \ud83d\ude80 When migrating my app from React Native CLI to Expo, I configured and performed an update via EAS.\nI noticed that the update file size was quite large, so I started investigating.\nInitially, I couldn't run , as I had to make some adjustments to Babel.\nOnce I managed to get it working, here\u2019s what I found.\nThe first step was to understand what was making the bundle so large.\nTo do this, I used , which allows visualizing the modules and dependencies that make up the bundle: This helped me identify unnecessary packages and heavy dependencies, here is the result: One of the biggest surprises was that Font Awesome was taking up a significant amount of space(6.39 MB).\nUpon further investigation, I realized that due to the way imports were structured, all icons were being included in the bundle, even though I was only using a small subset.\nInstead of using this: This ensures that only that specific icon will be added to the bundle, allowing tree-shaking to remove unused icons.\nAfter making these import changes, I ran again, and the results were impressive: 4.\nWhere Else Could I Reduce More?\nAs I continued analyzing, I noticed a package called .\nI had no idea what it was or why it was included in the project.\nAfter digging deeper, I discovered that this package had been deprecated for over six years.\nI then checked which package was still using it and found that depended on To resolve this, I looked into the library\u2019s GitHub repository and found that in newer versions, this dependency had been removed.\nSo, I updated to the latest version in my project.\nAfter implementing these changes, I ran again, and the final results were: I achieved a total reduction of ~70% in my React Native app\u2019s bundle size.\nAfter applying these optimizations, my app\u2019s bundle size was reduced by 70%, resulting in a lighter and more efficient application.\nThis improvement not only positively impacted the user experience but also reduced download time and storage usage on the device.\nIf you are also facing issues with bundle size, I recommend checking your imports and dependencies to identify unnecessary bloat!",
